To create a stacked area chart of sales over time by category, we can use the following Vega-Lite specification.

```json
{
  "$schema": "https://vega.github.io/schema/vega-lite/v5.json",
  "data": {
    "url": "your_data.csv"
  },
  "mark": {
    "type": "area",
    "interpolate": "monotone"
  },
  "encoding": {
    "x": {
      "field": "month",
      "type": "temporal"
    },
    "y": {
      "field": "sales_gbp",
      "type": "quantitative",
      "stack": true
    },
    "color": {
      "field": "category",
      "type": "nominal"
    }
  }
}
```

Replace `"your_data.csv"` with the actual URL or path to your CSV file. This specification assumes that the `month` column is in a format that can be parsed as a date. If it's not, you may need to add a `format` property to the `x` encoding to specify the date format.

To sort the stack so the largest category sits at the bottom, we need to sort the categories by their total sales in descending order. We can achieve this by adding a sort property to the color encoding.

```json
{
  "$schema": "https://vega.github.io/schema/vega-lite/v5.json",
  "data": {
    "url": "your_data.csv"
  },
  "mark": {
    "type": "area",
    "interpolate": "monotone"
  },
  "encoding": {
    "x": {
      "field": "month",
      "type": "temporal"
    },
    "y": {
      "field": "sales_gbp",
      "type": "quantitative",
      "stack": true
    },
    "color": {
      "field": "category",
      "type": "nominal",
      "sort": {
        "op": "sum",
        "field": "sales_gbp",
        "order": "descending"
      }
    }
  }
}
```

